+#### Remote answers to standard dialogs
+
+Interactive mode exposes all standard extension `select`, `confirm`, `input`, and `editor` dialogs through a pi-owned registry. The native desktop dialog remains available. Custom components, built-in dialogs, authentication prompts, and the restricted `project_trust` UI are not exposed. In RPC, JSON, and print modes these registry methods return an empty snapshot, a no-op unsubscribe function, and `"stale"` respectively. The existing RPC dialog protocol is unchanged.
+
+```typescript
+import type { DialogAnswer, DialogEvent, PendingDialog } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
+
+const pending = new Map<string, PendingDialog>();
+const unsubscribe = ctx.ui.onDialog((event: DialogEvent) => {
+  if (event.type === "closed") pending.delete(event.id);
+  else pending.set(event.dialog.id, event.dialog);
+});
+// Subscribe first, then take the snapshot. Deduplicate by ID.
+for (const dialog of ctx.ui.getPendingDialogs()) pending.set(dialog.id, dialog);
+
+// After authenticating and authorizing the remote caller:
+const answer: DialogAnswer = { kind: "confirm", value: true };
+const status = ctx.ui.resolveDialog(dialogId, answer);
+// "accepted" | "stale" | "invalid"
+```
+
+`PendingDialog` has a process-unique `id`, `title`, `kind`, and optional `timeout` in milliseconds. Select dialogs include `options`; confirmations include `message`; inputs include optional `placeholder`; editors include optional `prefill`. Snapshots and payloads are read-only. Events are `{ type: "opened" | "updated", dialog }` or `{ type: "closed", id }`. They do not include submitted answers.
+
+Answers use the matching `kind` and a `value`: boolean for confirmations, string for select/input/editor. Select values must match an offered option. Empty input/editor strings are valid. `{ kind: "cancel" }` returns `undefined` to select/input/editor callers and `false` to confirm callers.
+
+Pi mounts the desktop component before queuing `opened`. Desktop submission, cancellation, timeout, caller abort, and remote resolution settle through the same synchronous callback. The first valid answer wins; unknown or already closed IDs return `"stale"`. Wrong kinds or values return `"invalid"` without closing the dialog. Invalid answers never reserve a winner. Overlapping standard dialogs reject with `Error("Extension dialog busy")` before mounting, rather than replacing the active dialog. Await each dialog before opening another.
+
+Listeners run asynchronously without blocking dialog mounting or settlement; errors are ignored. Notifications already queued can arrive after unsubscribe or after settlement, in lifecycle order. Keep listeners short and reconcile with `getPendingDialogs()` after reconnect. Reload, session replacement, and stop cancel pending dialogs and clear listeners. Subscribe again in the new `session_start`. The notification-only `ui_prompt_start` / `ui_prompt_end` events retain their existing coalesced waiting-span behavior.
+
+**External editor limitation:** Ctrl+G temporarily gives an external process control of the desktop terminal. While it runs, the editor record has `unavailable: "external-editor"`, and an `updated` event reports that state. Every remote answer, including cancel, returns `"invalid"` until the external editor exits. Then another `updated` event clears `unavailable`, and the same ID accepts answers again. A mobile client must disable answering while unavailable. This is not full remote parity during external editing. The normal multiline TUI editor accepts remote answers. Editors do not gain timeout or AbortSignal options through this API. Reset invalidates the ID but does not terminate the external child; new standard dialogs remain busy until that child exits.
+
+The registry is an in-process extension API, not a network endpoint or authentication mechanism. A bridge must authenticate callers, authorize access to this session, and protect dialog titles, choices, messages, and prefills as potentially sensitive data. Unsubscribe and close transport resources in `session_shutdown`.
+
 #### Timed Dialogs with Countdown
 
 Dialogs support a `timeout` option that auto-dismisses with a live countdown display:
